                <pb facs="0539" n="445" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg445"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">612.112/74: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Warren</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-03-26T13:00:00-07:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 26,
                                1924—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 9:32 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>106. Foreign Office confirms the issuance of circular dated March 21st by General
                    Director of Customs, the second and third paragraphs of which read as follows:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <list>
                            <item>“[Second.] No form of customs’ duties ordinary, extraordinary or
                                port, or of penalties or pecuniary sanctions paid by parties to the
                                rebels will be demanded again by customs offices.</item>
                            <item>Third. The period of rebel control of ports shall not be taken
                                into account in the calculation of legal periods of any
                                kind.”</item>
                        </list>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Warren</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
